BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:PhD Dissertation Defense: Santiago Mille Fragoso
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T210000
DTSTAMP:20261018T061310Z
UID:5b66d55cf499ef46@gid-seminars.wnprc.wisc.edu
CATEGORIES:PhD Defense
DESCRIPTION:Title: Computational and Synthetic Design of Modular Molecular
  Devices\n\nAbstract: Cells use molecular mechanisms to detect environment
 al signals and convert them into functional responses. Engineering this ca
 pability could enable programmable cell behaviors\, diagnostics\, and ther
 apies\, yet these sensors remain difficult to adapt to new inputs and outp
 uts. I propose new strategies for designing molecular devices\, introducin
 g methods to design binders to proteins of interest (POIs) and a post-tran
 scriptional mechanism that converts the binding of a molecule into an outp
 ut\, thereby contributing complementary approaches to program the sensing
 \, processing\, and actuation layers of synthetic molecular sensors. First
 \, I present LIDAR (Ligand-Induced Dimerization Activating RNA editing)\, 
 a post-transcriptional receptor platform that couples ligand-dependent pro
 tein proximity to ADAR-mediated RNA editing. LIDAR functions across archit
 ectures and ligand classes to produce functional outputs. Second\, I prese
 nt Germinal\, a computational pipeline for designing antibodies for therap
 eutic and synthetic biology applications. By jointly optimizing antibody s
 tructure and sequence with AlphaFold-Multimer and an antibody language mod
 el\, I generated VHH and scFv binders against four proteins using tens of 
 tested designs per target\, with structural and mutational analyses confir
 ming their binding modes. Finally\, I outline how these advances could con
 verge in LIDAR-inspired synthetic receptors that couple antibody-based rec
 ognition of intracellular proteins to RNA editing. Together\, these two pr
 ojects establish a path toward modular molecular devices whose inputs and 
 outputs can be selected by design.\n\nPlease contact Sofia Rakicevic-More 
 for the Zoom link.\n\nEvent URL: https://events.stanford.edu/event/phd-dis
 sertation-defense-santiago-mille-fragoso\n\nSource: stanford_virtual
LOCATION:Clark Center\, S360
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/phd-dissertation-defense-santiago-mi
 lle-fragoso
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Reminder: PhD Dissertation Defense: Santiago Mille Fragoso
TRIGGER:-PT1H
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
