SUMMARY:Alcohol-Associated Hepatitis and Early Liver Transplantation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261111T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261111T210000
DTSTAMP:20261018T061310Z
UID:9f6697f7b28f9a6e@gid-seminars.wnprc.wisc.edu
CATEGORIES:Class/Seminar
DESCRIPTION:OverviewThis activity reviews the diagnosis and management of 
 alcohol-associated liver disease (ALD) and alcohol-associated hepatitis (A
 AH)\, including alcohol cessation strategies\, treatment of alcohol use di
 sorder\, appropriate use of corticosteroids\, and the evolving role of ear
 ly liver transplantation. Learners will gain practical guidance on risk st
 ratification\, treatment thresholds\, and timely referral of patients with
  severe alcohol-associated liver disease.\n\nRegistrationRegistration for 
 all practitioners - free.\n\nTo register for this activity\, please click 
 HERE\n\nCreditsAMA PRA Category 1 Credits™ (1.00 hours)\, AAPA Category 
 1 CME credits (1.00 hours)\, ANCC Contact Hours (1.00 hours)\, Non-Physici
 an Participation Credit (1.00 hours)\n\nTarget AudienceSpecialties - Famil
 y Medicine & Community Health\, Gastroenterology\, Gastroenterology & Hepa
 tology\, Hematology\, Internal MedicineProfessions - Advance Practice Nurs
 e (APN)\, Non-Physician\, Nurse\, Physician\, Physician Associate\, Regist
 ered Nurse (RN) ObjectivesAt the conclusion of this activity\, learners sh
 ould be able to:\n1. Apply NIAAA clinical criteria and phosphatidylethanol
  testing to accurately diagnose alcohol-associated hepatitis and distingui
 sh alcohol-associated liver disease from MASLD.\n2. Use MELD rather than t
 he Maddrey discriminant function to risk-stratify patients with alcohol-as
 sociated hepatitis and identify candidates for corticosteroid therapy.\n3.
  Manage corticosteroid therapy appropriately\, including assessment of con
 traindications and calculation of the Lille score at day 4 or 7 to discont
 inue treatment in non-responders.\n4. Initiate concurrent treatment of alc
 ohol use disorder using behavioral support and evidence-based pharmacother
 apy\, avoiding agents contraindicated in liver disease.\n5. Identify patie
 nts appropriate for early liver transplant referral without applying a fix
 ed 6-month sobriety interval\, and document eligibility using specific\, n
 on-stigmatizing language.\n\nAccreditationIn support of\n\nEvent URL: http
 s://events.stanford.edu/event/alcohol-associated-hepatitis-and-early-liver
 -transplantation\n\nSource: stanford_virtual
LOCATION:Online
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/alcohol-associated-hepatitis-and-ear
 ly-liver-transplantation
